# Why do incentive schemes backfire?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What happens when you pay for what you can measure? Multitasking and Goodhart's law"
next: {"title":"Who should own the firm?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/117-who-should-own-the-firm.cast"}

## Hitting the target
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 2000–01
      - 2001–02
      - 2002–03
      - 2003–04
      - 2004–05
    values:
      - 3
      - 13
      - 17
      - 22
      - 26
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 32
    y_label: Services meeting the target
    x_label: English ambulance services
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 150
      w: 450
      h: 480
sources:
    - id: bevanhamblin
      title: 'Hitting and missing targets by ambulance services for emergency calls: effects of different systems of performance measurement within the UK'
      authors: Bevan, G. & Hamblin, R.
      year: 2009
      finding: 'Journal of the Royal Statistical Society A 172(1): 161–190. Target: 75% of category A calls reached within 8 minutes. Table 3 (Department of Health''s published annual figures): English trusts meeting it: 3 (of 32) in 2000–01, the year before star ratings; then 13, 17, 22 and 26 (of 31 after mergers) in 2001–02 to 2004–05. Bulges and one dramatic spike just under 8 minutes; manual ''corrections'' in around a third of trusts, worth at most 6% (CHI); ''to get there in 8.01 minutes and save the patient is seen as a failure'' (a common view, CHI 2003); some hospitals kept patients waiting in ambulances outside to meet the 4-hour A&E target.'
      doi: 10.1111/j.1467-985X.2008.00557.x
    - id: bevanhood
      title: 'What''s measured is what matters: targets and gaming in the English public health care system'
      authors: Bevan, G. & Hood, C.
      year: 2006
      finding: 'Public Administration 84(3): 517–538. Governance by targets assumes the measured part can stand for the whole and that gaming does not matter; evidence of gaming from the English NHS.'
      doi: 10.1111/j.1467-9299.2006.00600.x

    text target "Target: 3 in 4 urgent calls\nreached within 8 min" #2f6b8f cites ["bevanhamblin"] x 775 y 520 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch found cites ["bevanhamblin"] work ["spike just under 8:00","~1 in 3: times fixed by hand","8:01 + saved = “failure”","patients held in ambulances"] x 775 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true
    text lesson "“What's measured\nis what matters”" #b5482e cites ["bevanhood"] x 775 y 470 font_size 40 hidden true
    text lesson_by "Bevan & Hood, 2006" #8f887c x 775 y 370 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Hitting the target"

Why do incentive schemes so often backfire? Pay people for a number, and you get the number. Whether you get what you wanted is another matter.
    draw axes

B: Take England's ambulance services around (@draw target@) the year 2000. The target: reach three in four of the most urgent calls within eight minutes.

On the Department of Health's yearly figures, in 2000 to 2001, the yea (@draw bar_1@)r before services were graded on it, only three of thirty-two hit it.

    ask "How many of the 31 hit the target in 2004–05? Drag the bar." on bar_5 store g default 8 gap false voice b
        intro: Then the target went into public star ratings, with managers' jobs on the line, and mergers left thirty-one services. How many hit it four years later? Drag the last bar.
        right: {g.true:0}: you said {g:0}. Well judged.
        wrong: You said {g:0}: here, {g.true:0}.

Twenty-six. Three, then  (@draw bar_2@)thirteen, sevente (@draw bar_3@)en, twenty-two, tw (@draw bar_4@)enty-six: a target, a league table, and the numbers soared.

    pause 0.3

B: So targets work?
    erase target

Partly. When inspectors plotted the response times, some services (@draw found_box found_line_1@) showed a bulge, and one a sharp spike, just under eight minutes.

B: And in about a thi (@draw found_line_2@)rd of services, times had been corrected by hand after the fact. Inspectors reckoned that flattered the results by at most six pe (@highlight bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5@)rcent, so most of the climb was real.

But the target had changed what counted as success. One report noted a common view: get the (@draw found_line_3@)re in eight minutes and one second, save the patient, and it is a failure.

B: And hospitals had a four-hour target in emergency departments. Some kept (@draw found_line_4@) patients waiting in ambulances outside until they could be seen within the four hours.

    erase found

Gwyn Bevan and Christopher Hood  (@draw lesson lesson_by@)put the lesson in a title: what's measured is what matters.

So here's the puzzle. The incenti (@highlight bar_5 effect box@)ve worked on the number. Why did it also bend ever (@highlight lesson effect underline@)ything the number left out?

## Paying for what you count
vars:
    b:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 300
      label: Bonus per hull ($)
    ef:
      expr: '1'
    hs:
      expr: 4 + 4*b/(b + 100)
    hc:
      expr: 8 - hs
    Q:
      expr: 100*ef*hs/4
    V:
      expr: 100*ef*hs*(8 - hs)/16
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Speed (counted)
      - Care (unseen)
    values:
      - 4
      - 4
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: ' h'
      decimals: 1
    colors:
      - red
      - purple
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 8
    y_label: Sara's hours a day
    label_size: 26
    reference:
      value: 4
      color: grey
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 150
      w: 460
      h: 480
bind_params: {"values.0":"hs","values.1":"hc"}
sources:
    - id: hm1991
      title: 'Multitask principal-agent analyses: incentive contracts, asset ownership, and job design'
      authors: Holmström, B. & Milgrom, P.
      year: 1991
      finding: 'Journal of Law, Economics, & Organization 7 (special issue): 24–52. When tasks compete for the agent''s time, incentives for one activity are less desirable the harder the others are to measure; an optimal contract can be a fixed wage. Motivating example: teachers paid on test scores.'
      doi: 10.1093/jleo/7.special_issue.24
    - id: kerr1975
      title: On the folly of rewarding A, while hoping for B
      authors: Kerr, S.
      year: 1975
      finding: 'Academy of Management Journal 18(4): 769–783. Reward systems often reward behaviour other than what the rewarder hopes for; e.g. universities reward research while hoping professors will also teach well.'
    - id: strathern1997
      title: '''Improving ratings'': audit in the British University system'
      authors: Strathern, M.
      year: 1997
      finding: 'European Review 5(3): 305–321. The common phrasing of Goodhart''s law: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure (Goodhart''s own 1975 version concerned monetary statistics).'
    - id: weitzman1980
      title: The 'ratchet principle' and performance incentives
      authors: Weitzman, M. L.
      year: 1980
      finding: 'Bell Journal of Economics 11(1): 302–308. When next period''s target is raised by this period''s performance, agents restrain performance.'

    text sara "Sara, welder\n8 hours a day" #3d3833 x 790 y 500 font_size 34 hidden true
    portrait hb cites ["hm1991"] x 800 y 470 width 190 of "Bengt Holmström" cameo true hidden true
    text r_q "Hulls counted: {Q:0}" #b5482e x 790 y 520 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r_v "Real value: {V:0}" #3d3833 x 790 y 440 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r_note "flat wage = 100" #8f887c x 790 y 380 font_size 24 hidden true
    text flat_note "flat wage\nmay be best" #2f6b8f cites ["hm1991"] x 790 y 470 font_size 38 hidden true
    text kerr "rewarding A,\nhoping for B" #8a5fa8 cites ["kerr1975"] x 790 y 470 font_size 38 hidden true
    text kerr_by "Kerr, 1975" #8f887c x 790 y 390 font_size 24 hidden true
    text goodhart "measure → target:\nno longer a\ngood measure" #b5482e cites ["strathern1997"] x 790 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true
    text ratchet "+ the ratchet:\nhold back" #8f887c cites ["weitzman1980"] x 790 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch mathcard work ["pay = w + b × speed + c × care","b > c: hours drift to speed","so set b = c","care unmeasurable: c = 0, so b = 0"] x 560 y 430 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Paying for what you count"

Targets bend what they leave out. To see why, take the principal–agent problem again, now with one worker and two jobs.
    draw axes

B: Say Sara welds hulls at Harbourtown's boatyard, eigh (@draw sara@)t hours a day, with illustrative numbers throughout. One part of her job is speed: hulls finished, which the yard counts.

    pause 0.2

The other part is care: we (@draw reference_1@)lds that still hold in five years. Nobody can see that today.

B: On a flat wage, say she splits her day evenly: four hours each.
    highlight reference_1

    erase sara

    ask "Paid per hull, nothing for care: how do you split your 8 hours? Set both bars." on all budget 8 judge false account_label "Your 8 hours" default 4,4 store d gap false
        intro: Now the yard pays a bonus for every hull, and nothing for care. You're Sara: split your eight hours.
        right: Here is yours beside the flat-wage four and four. Now the model.

It moves two hours: six on speed, two on care.
    animate b 100 duration 2 ghost true

In 1991 Bengt Holmströ (@draw hb@)m and Paul Milgrom worked out what happens: when two tasks compete for your time, paying for one pulls hours out of the other.

    erase hb

B: So the  (@draw r_q r_v r_note@)count climbs, while the boats, all things considered, get worse.

Try it: drag the bonus up, and watch counted hulls climb while the real value of the work sinks.
    explore params ["b"]

    erase r_q r_v r_note

Their striking conclusion: if the other task can't be measured, the best c (@draw flat_note@)ontract may be a flat wage, with no bonus at all.
    animate b 0 duration 2

    erase flat_note

B: Management writers had a name for this already. In 1975 Steven Kerr  (@draw kerr kerr_by@)called it the folly of rewarding A, while hoping for B.

His example: universities that reward research, while hoping their professors will also teach well.
    highlight kerr

    erase kerr kerr_by

B: And once people aim at the number itself, the number stops telling you anything. Goodhart's law, (@draw goodhart@) in Marilyn Strathern's words: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

Add the ratche (@draw ratchet@)t, the trap Soviet planners knew well: beat this year's target by a lot, and next year's goes up. So the smart worker holds back.

    erase goodhart ratchet

    animate b 100 duration 1.5

So pay pulls effort towards whatever is counted, and away from whatever is not.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: Want the reason in symbols?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes bar_1 bar_2 reference_1 to 0.2

Pay her a wage w, plus b per unit of the counted task and c per unit of the other, with eight hours to share.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

If b is bigger than c, every hour moved to the counted task pays more, so the hours keep drifting there.
    draw mathcard_line_2

So the two rewards have to match.
    draw mathcard_line_3

And if the second task can't be measured, c is zero, so b must be zero too: a flat wage.
    draw mathcard_line_4

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq

    fade axes bar_1 bar_2 reference_1 to 1

## When pay works
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Holmström and Milgrom would hire you to design their bonus scheme.
    good: You can smell a gamed target, mostly.
    poor: Ask what the count leaves out, and the answers follow.
    none: 'Pay is loud: next time, listen for what it leaves out.'
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Hourly wage
      - Pay per windshield
    values:
      - 100
      - 144
    value_labels: true
    colors:
      - null
      - green
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 160
    y_label: Output per worker (index)
    x_label: Safelite Glass, 1994–95
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 150
      w: 460
      h: 480
sources:
    - id: lazear2000
      title: Performance pay and productivity
      authors: Lazear, E. P.
      year: 2000
      finding: 'American Economic Review 90(5): 1346–1361. Safelite Glass switched installers from hourly wages to piece rates in 1994–95; output per worker rose 44% and profits rose; part of the gain is incentive (the same workers producing more), part sorting (more able workers hired and retained). Data on about 3,000 workers over 19 months.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.90.5.1346
    - id: lazear1996
      title: Performance pay and productivity (NBER Working Paper 5672)
      authors: Lazear, E. P.
      year: 1996
      finding: A broken windshield is traced to its installer, who must reinstall it on his own time and pay for the replacement glass. 'Output is easily measured and quality problems are detected and blame is assignable. Managerial and professional jobs may not be as well suited to piecework.'
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w5672
    - id: hm1991b
      title: 'Multitask principal-agent analyses: incentive contracts, asset ownership, and job design'
      authors: Holmström, B. & Milgrom, P.
      year: 1991
      finding: 'Journal of Law, Economics, & Organization 7: 24–52. Incentives for one task are less desirable the harder competing tasks are to measure.'
      doi: 10.1093/jleo/7.special_issue.24

    scratch why cites ["lazear1996"] work ["same people, working harder","faster fitters came and stayed","broken? refit it on your own time","easy to count, easy to blame"] x 790 y 450 font_size 25 hidden true
    text agreed "unpaid tasks suffer:\nmostly agreed" #2f6b8f cites ["hm1991b"] x 780 y 520 font_size 34 hidden true
    text contested "how much a target harms:\ncontested" #b5482e x 780 y 380 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "When pay works"

So pay pulls effort towards what is counted. Then is a bonus always a mistake?
    draw axes

B: Not at Safelite Glass, an American firm that fits car windshields. In 1994 and 1995 (@draw bar_1@) it switched its fitters from an hourly wage to pay per windshield.

    ask "Hourly wage = 100. Drag the bar to output per worker on pay per windshield." on bar_2 store g default 110
        intro: The economist Edward Lazear studied the records of about three thousand fitters. Call output per worker on the hourly wage a hundred. Guess what it became.
        right: {g.true:0}: you said {g:0}. Well judged.
        wrong: You said {g:0}: here, {g.true:0}.

B: A hundred and forty-four. Forty-four percent more per worker, and the firm's profits rose too.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

Part of it c (@draw why_box why_line_1@)ame from the same people working harder, part from who j (@draw why_line_2@)oined and who stayed: faster fitters liked the new pay.

B: And why no backfire? Because care was counted too.  (@draw why_line_3@)A windshield that broke came back to its fitter, to refit on his own time, paying for the glass.

Lazear's own caution: here outp (@draw why_line_4@)ut is easy to count, and the blame for a bad job is easy to assign. Managers' and professionals' jobs are rarely like that.

    erase why

How sure are we? That unpaid tasks suffer when they compete for time with  (@draw agreed ends@)paid ones: mostly agreed.

B: How much harm a given target does, in a hospital, a school or a boatyard, and whether a better m (@draw contested ends@)easure fixes it: contested, case by case.

    erase agreed contested

    hide axes bar_1 bar_2

    cards sortc bins ["Works well","Likely to backfire"] check each
        * "Fruit picker, per crate" bin "Works well"
        * "Typist, per error-free page" bin "Works well"
        * "Fitter, redoes own breaks" bin "Works well"
        * "Call agent, per call ended" bin "Likely to backfire"
        * "Police, per arrest" bin "Likely to backfire"
        * "Doctor, per test ordered" bin "Likely to backfire"

    ask "Paying per piece: where does it work well, and where is it likely to backfire? Sort each card." intro "Now some new cases." on sortc store f stage own voice b
        right: All six: where the count captures what matters, pay per piece works.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total}: calls, arrests, tests backfire.

    erase sortc

    text leftout "What does the\ncount leave out?" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 420 font_size 48

    quiz "A council pays road crews per pothole filled. The likely result?"
        right: Quick patches that break up again: the count rewards speed, and the patch's life goes unpaid.
        wrong: What does the count leave out? Here, how long the patch lasts.
        * Fewer potholes filled
        + Quick patches that break up again
        * Crews refuse the bonus

    quiz "A saleswoman’s bonus is for beating last year's sales, and by November she already has. What might she do in December?" voice b
        right: Push some sales into January: this year's bonus is safe, and a huge December would only raise next year's bar.
        wrong: Think about next year's target, and the ratchet: here, January.
        * Sell even harder
        + Push some sales into January
        * Nothing changes

    erase leftout

    show axes bar_1 bar_2

So why do incentive schemes backfire? Because pay is loud. It tells people what counts, and they listen to the number, not to what you meant.
    highlight bar_2

